> # The shared probe budget is set ONCE before the loop. SECONDS is whole-second
> # granularity, so `SECONDS + N` expires at the next integer tick -- between
> # (N-1, N]s after this assignment, depending on the sub-second phase when it runs.
> #
> # v30 FIX (budget only): this was `SECONDS + 1` through v28/v29, giving a budget in
> # (0,1]s -- and at a whole-second boundary phase it collapses toward 0. Measured
> # 2026-09-06 (v29 breadcrumb soak, 1 hit in 1500 launches at 16 ms; independently
> # re-derived by a pure-builtin `SECONDS+1` spin, ~1.5% expiring <20 ms): the
> # watchdog then killed the first interpreter probe before it could exit 37, no
> # worker spawned, the event silently lost -- the `D_pid_INT` VOID. `+ 2` moves the
> # floor: the budget is now (1,2]s, so its MINIMUM exceeds 1 s regardless of phase,
> # and a python startup (~30-100 ms here) can no longer be clipped by an unlucky
> # tick. Cost: worst-case probe phase grows from ~1s to ~2s. This is the SAME whole-
> # second source and the SAME (kill-at-tick) mechanism as before -- only the budget
> # WIDTH changed -- so every round-9..27 property of the loop below is preserved; it
> # is deliberately NOT a new sub-second clock (bash 3.2 has none) or a poll-iteration
> # counter (which would re-introduce the clip via a fast-returning `/bin/sleep`).
> # The worker watchdog is a SEPARATE `SECONDS + DEADLINE` (DEADLINE=2) whose floor is
> # already >1s; no VOID was ever traced to it, so v30 leaves it UNCHANGED (scope).
> # See ~/.claude/projects/.../memory/finding_harness_rerun_void_2026-09-06.md.
> # The C3 bound still holds: the probe phase is < 2s (SECONDS+2) and the worker
> # watchdog is a SEPARATE fresh DEADLINE=2 AFTER it, so the hook is bounded by
> # their sum (~4s worst case), not by a single DEADLINE=2 shared with the worker.
